void operator delete(void* ptr) noexcept {
    if (!ptr) return;
    AllocHeader* hdr = static_cast<AllocHeader*>(
        static_cast<uint8_t*>(ptr) - sizeof(AllocHeader));

    if (hdr->magic != 0xA110C8ED) {
        log_error("Heap corruption or bad free");
        return;
        // Or halt() on a safety-critical ECU
    }

    record_free(hdr->sequence, hash);
    free(hdr);
}
```

## Associating allocations with call sites

You're right that a raw count doesn't point at a culprit. The trick is **allocation-site tracking**: group allocations by *where* they came from, not just when.

### Option A: `__builtin_return_address(0)` — cheapest, works well

GCC and Clang give you the return address of the caller inside your overridden `operator new`. Capture it at allocation time:

```cpp
void* operator new(size_t size) {
    void* caller = __builtin_return_address(0); // PC of whoever called new
    // store `caller` in the AllocHeader alongside size/sequence
}
```

